# settings.local.R for SciNet Ceres.
#
#   cp container/settings.local.R.scinet settings.local.R
#
# VALUES ONLY. The store and cache paths, and the guards that keep them on node-local disk,
# come from cluster_scratch_paths() in the TRACKED settings.R. That split matters: this file is
# gitignored, so `git pull` never updates it, and a copy made earlier would keep whatever rules
# were current then -- silently. Keeping the logic in settings.R means fixes reach you.
#
# Read cluster_scratch_paths() if you want to know why db_path lands where it does; the short
# version is that SQLite's WAL needs genuinely local disk, which inside a Ceres job is $TMPDIR.

settings_override <- c(

  cluster_scratch_paths(),

  list(
    # ---- sizing ----------------------------------------------------------
    # Memory is the binding resource: measured 19 GB median, 82 GB peak per worker. Raise only
    # after tools/report_memory.R shows headroom on a real Ceres run.
    dosage_budget_bytes = 16e9
  )
)

# NO WALL-CLOCK SETTING HERE -- the job is meant to be killed at its wall clock, which is
# declared once, in submit.local.sh. A clean stop would buy nothing worth having:
#   * Both backups run every 30 min from WHICHEVER worker gets there first, so a hard kill
#     costs at most one interval.
#   * Nothing is corrupted by it. backup_store() writes dest.tmp<PID> and renames, so a killed
#     backup leaves the previous good one; and SQLite in WAL mode is crash-safe by design.
#   * A self-imposed stop is only tested BETWEEN evaluations, so it cannot save the in-flight
#     one -- that is lost either way.
# Against that, a margin makes every worker idle for it (22 workers x 0.5 h = 11 worker-hours a
# job), and it cannot know whether the next evaluation needs 29 minutes or 33 hours.
#
# To end an interactive run, use the stop-file: touch $OPTIMIZER_HOME/state/STOP.
